P&amp;L and average return portrayed are not available in the current data. Our AI analysis is learning

00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:08.060
from patterns around catalysts fade and adjusting entries accordingly. Now here's something that

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:13.880
most retail investors get wrong and it cost me money before I figured it out. Imagine you added a

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:20.880
Canadian bank stock to your TFSA last quarter expecting a 4% yield. Your brokerage shows the dividend

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:27.200
credited but the actual after-tax advantage only appears when you compare eligible Canadian dividends

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:34.200
against foreign ones inside versus outside registered accounts. Eligible Canadian dividends

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:40.560
receive a gross up and dividend tax credit that eliminates tax inside a TFSA or RRSP,

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:48.080
while US dividends still face 15% withholding even in those accounts. Most retail investors overlook

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:54.100
that the credit is wasted in registered accounts, yet becomes valuable in non-registered accounts.

00:06:54.760 --> 00:07:01.120
The pro tip is to run a quick comparison in your brokerage's tax estimator. Move any US dividend

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:06.880
payers into an RRSP where withholding is reclamable via treaty and keep Canadian dividend

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:13.500
aristocrats in the TFSA. The biggest mistake with dividend placement is treating all yields as

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:19.300
equal. Instead, always map the tax character of each holding to the account type before the next

00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:25.920
contribution deadline. Before we wrap, keep an eye on whether financial ETF volume confirms any

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:31.660
